TITLE:: PltNovelty
summary:: Spectral novelty with the detection threshold and the onsets it picks
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/Onsets, Classes/PltTrack, Classes/PltSpectrogram

DESCRIPTION::
The curve an onset detector actually looks at, with the threshold drawn across it
and the detections as marks along the top. A missed hit or a doubled trigger stops
being mysterious.

Only increases in magnitude count. A spectrum falling away is not an onset, and
counting the whole difference makes every note ending look like a note starting.

In live mode the server's own link::Classes/Onsets:: decisions are drawn along the
bottom in a second colour, so its behaviour can be compared with the curve.

note::
Novelty is normalised to its own maximum, so the threshold reads 0 to 1 whatever the
material. Live, the maximum decays slowly, so a loud passage does not flatten
everything after it.
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: fromSoundFile
A whole file, offline, from the same frames link::Classes/PltSpectrogram:: computes.
argument:: path
argument:: fftSize
argument:: hop
Fraction of a window between frames.
argument:: threshold
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: live
From a bus, with the server's Onsets drawn alongside.
argument:: bus
argument:: fftSize
argument:: server
argument:: threshold
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: flux
Novelty per frame: the sum of increases in magnitude, normalised to its maximum.

METHOD:: peaks
Frames above the threshold that are also local maxima, with anything inside
code::minGapFrames:: of the last pick suppressed. A plain threshold crossing fires
several times on one attack, which is what a doubled onset is.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: onsetTimes
The onsets in seconds, offset to the middle of the analysis window. A frame is named
by where it starts, and a 1024 point window at 48 kHz starts 11 ms before the middle
of what it heard, which reported every click that much too early.

METHOD:: threshold
Setting it repicks the onsets, so a threshold can be dragged and the result watched.

METHOD:: flux
METHOD:: onsets
Frame indices.
METHOD:: minGap
Seconds of suppression after a pick.
METHOD:: span
Seconds visible, live.
METHOD:: pollRate
METHOD:: duration
METHOD:: frameRate
METHOD:: sampleRate
METHOD:: reset
METHOD:: start
METHOD:: stop
METHOD:: pushFrame
One frame of magnitudes, from anywhere.
METHOD:: fluxColorKey
METHOD:: onsetColorKey
METHOD:: serverColorKey

EXAMPLES::
code::
// a file, with the threshold visible
n = PltNovelty.fromSoundFile(Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav").front;
n.threshold_(0.4);      // fewer onsets, repicked at once
n.threshold_(0.15);     // more

// live, with the server's own decisions along the bottom
s.boot;
n = PltNovelty.live(0).front;
x = Pbind(\dur, 0.3, \freq, Pwhite(200, 800), \sustain, 0.05).play;
x.stop; n.close;

// data only: the flux of a spectrogram's frames
(
var a = PltSpectrogram.framesFromSoundFile(
	Platform.resourceDir +/+ "sounds/a11wlk01.wav", 1024, 0.25);
var f = PltNovelty.flux(a[\frames]);
PltNovelty.peaks(f, 0.3, 4).size;
)
::
